- Horizontally shift it by $3$ units to the right to give $y={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x-3}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(3,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/3a4e3b5a90900d204c3265187d01343233bcaa76d811276464742fa447e4543a.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x-3}.$ The point $(3,1)$ moves to $(3,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/0936975d549f0f94f4c4139b9543142308c2cc56d38d1bba2c94dc6c681b3d99.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fa86dd3-3bb3-54d5-8940-5dc07ae4c99f" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Growth Functions With Horizontal and Vertical Shifts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4236502f-426b-5ad3-ab08-c50996b03ce0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/559d2a03eeafc73b1ed1c8e98cb7380ea4ed0e956addfee31ed7412632133e80.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288166} {:answer/id #uuid "342357eb-9230-5867-bb6e-4bc7acb0332c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/b669aa5f756a95886182ef6d71c06fc3322192d52f18755603ef3dead48ee309.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288167} {:answer/id #uuid "3db9dad3-024f-5453-9aa4-adbad5e84862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/4c65c1b20467750125f3e25eb598544d4134d353c72e8bd60b48556d995fa2c7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288168} {:answer/id #uuid "ef888a9b-b99d-52a0-a9ea-046dbb011b9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/D